{
  "updated": "2026-07-25",
  "title": "STUDIO",
  "intro": "a practice of creating and chasing after images that intrigue me.",
  "series": [
    {
      "id": "poster-studies",
      "numeral": "I",
      "name": "Poster Studies",
      "note": "Where the idea for starting the studio first came from. I've always been fascinated with Japanese designers of the 20th century, from magazines like IDEA and Urban Housing among others. These first poster studies came from thinking about visual aesthetics in procedural ways: questioning how each element of a composition can be made through a generative system, how randomness can explore a design's potential visual range. I am no artist, just someone inspired by the creativity of others.",
      "tags": [
        "print",
        "posters"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "broken-room",
      "numeral": "II",
      "name": "Spatial Studies",
      "note": "Experiments in creating spaces. Pushing what the idea of perspective lines can be. Many systems also acting as explorative processes of trying to create skeuomorphic paint systems through code. Trying to understand the ways our eyes perceive paint-like textures, and replicating those ideas through systems. It's the same general interest in thinking of the world in procedural ways, or as the incredible artist Iskra Velitchkova might say, \"I see generative.\" Each type of paint works in different manners and attempting to procedurally create things like the impasto of oils, matte-ness of gouaches, transparency of watercolors and more becomes fun visual puzzles.",
      "tags": [
        "rooms",
        "perspective"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "scores",
      "numeral": "III",
      "name": "Notation, Scores & Sound",
      "note": "Graphic notation as a working vocabulary and material for generative systems.",
      "tags": [
        "notation",
        "music"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "misc",
      "numeral": "IV",
      "name": "Misc. Studies",
      "note": "Standalone experiments around visual ideas and inspirations."
    }
  ],
