    {
      "slug": "multiform",
      "name": "MULTIFORM",
      "edition": null,
      "kind": "experiment",
      "year": 2026,
      "after": "Mark Rothko, the multiforms and after",
      "medium": "glazed rectangles over a painted ground",
      "status": "approved",
      "note": "This series is a great example of what can come from the emergence of exploring a system's output range. A colour-field plate generator that started as one output of the Fugitive atlas study I liked too much to leave behind. There's a constant discussion of \"well, you could never paint a Rothko\", in the sense that there's context, process and material behind Rothko's creations, and even if you paint overlaid squares that resemble one, it still won't carry the emotional feel of a true Rothko. I decided to take the one Fugitive output that reminded me of Rothko and drill into it anyway. Soft glazed rectangles over a fully painted canvas ground, with 21 palettes taken from real Rothko canvases across the lineage of his career. I explored the system's range using the same atlas tool as before and have been pleasantly surprised by the outputs.",
